//? Title: Hidden Markov Models: Sum Paths, Select Paths and Learn
//? Subtitle: The best hidden path is different from the sum of all path probabilities
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
A sequence of observed symbols can be generated by hidden states that evolve over time.
We compare total sequence likelihood, state posterior probabilities and the most likely path
on a four-symbol example with only sixteen possible paths. A visible Adam loop fits normalised tensor logits
by differentiating the observation likelihood, without hidden-state labels.
)
\toc

> The method

An HMM specifies initial masses pi, transition probabilities A and emission probabilities B.
A joint path probability multiplies one initial mass, each emission and each transition.
Forward inference sums over earlier states, while Viterbi replaces that sum with a maximum:

$$
\alpha_t(j)=B_{j,o_t}\sum_i\alpha_{t-1}(i)A_{ij},\qquad
\delta_t(j)=\log B_{j,o_t}+\max_i(\delta_{t-1}(i)+\log A_{ij}).
$$
\alttext(Forward recursion sums probability from all predecessor states; Viterbi recursion selects the best log-probability predecessor.)

The library forward recursion uses log-sum-exp to avoid underflow. The derivative of log
likelihood with respect to a time-specific log emission potential is its smoothed state posterior:
differentiating the sum over paths weights the indicator of that state by the posterior path mass.
For fitting, log_softmax normalises initial, transition and emission logits, and the visible Adam
loop minimises mean negative log likelihood. This direct gradient fit is distinct from Baum–Welch;
Adam has no per-step monotonicity guarantee and can reach a local optimum. Inference costs O(TK²) rather than enumerating K^T paths.
State names may permute. Posterior marginals need not form the Viterbi path, and the best path's
joint probability cannot exceed total sequence probability. All state inference and fitting use tensor operations. Tiny-path tensor enumeration independently
checks both the summed likelihood and the posterior; it also identifies the most likely path.

The concept diagram has a retained TikZ source in `diagrams/hidden_markov_models_concept.tikz`.
Regeneration requires LaTeX with PGF/TikZ and standalone, and dvisvgm.

```{bash, results="output", file="figures/hidden_markov_models_concept.svg"}
set -eu
mkdir -p build
latex -interaction=batchmode -halt-on-error -output-directory=build diagrams/hidden_markov_models_concept.tikz > build/hidden_markov_models_concept.compile.log 2>&1
dvisvgm --no-fonts --bbox=10 --output=build/hidden_markov_models_concept.svg build/hidden_markov_models_concept.dvi > build/hidden_markov_models_concept.render.log 2>&1
printf 'Rendered build/hidden_markov_models_concept.svg from local TikZ source.\n'
```
